      "content": "i get that it wasn't actually a good policy, but for the sake of the debate i need to be able to argue the \"logic\" that was used at the time. like, if i'm on the side trying to defend it, i can't just say 'it was racist'—that's not a debate strategy. \n\ncan u try to frame it more through the lens of the actual economic and social justifications used back then? like, what were the specific arguments for how it helped maintain neighborhood stability or protect investment? i just need the structural points so i can understand how someone would have defended it in a formal setting.",
